WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:07.840
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.629
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.629 --> 00:00:12.430
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.100
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.470
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.550
things about the world at the same

00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:20.340
time as improving your English.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:23.599
I'm Alastair Budge, and this is part

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:26.340
two of our three-part mini-series

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:29.250
on blasphemy and free expression.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:32.049
In part one, we traced the history

00:00:32.059 --> 00:00:34.420
of blasphemy in Britain, from that

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:36.639
20-year-old university student who was

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:38.210
hanged for saying the wrong things at

00:00:38.219 --> 00:00:40.970
dinner right through to the present day.

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.110
In parts two and three of this

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:45.360
mini-series, this episode and the next

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.620
one, we are going to talk about the

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:50.239
most famous blasphemy controversy of

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.670
the twentieth century: the story of

00:00:52.679 --> 00:00:55.360
Salman Rushdie and The Satanic Verses.

00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:58.490
It’s a story involving a best-selling

00:00:58.529 --> 00:01:01.640
author, questions of politics, identity

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:04.229
and religion, the Ayatollah of Iran,

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:07.400
a death sentence, an unsolved murder,

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:09.880
an attempted murder, and fundamental

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:12.780
questions about freedom of expression.

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.399
We’ve got a lot to talk about,

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:18.010
so let’s get right into it.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:22.410
Salman Rushdie had a reputation for

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.600
being something of a ladies’ man.

00:01:25.349 --> 00:01:27.590
He was a frequent feature on the

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:30.480
London dinner party circuit, and by

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.630
the late 1980s, he was already onto

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:37.000
his second of what would be five wives.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:39.330
Valentine's Day, then,

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:41.560
should have suited him well.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:43.270
It did not.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.630
It hadn’t started particularly well;

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.890
he had gone to the funeral of his great

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:53.173
friend, the travel writer Bruce Chatwin.

00:01:53.959 --> 00:01:56.839
And later on that day, things

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.750
turned from bad to worse.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:03.050
He was at a friend's house when

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:04.630
someone turned on the radio.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:08.990
The Supreme Leader of Iran, Ayatollah

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:11.970
Ruhollah Khomeini, had issued a

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.870
fatwa, a death sentence, against

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:17.710
someone he deemed his opponent.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:21.230
This was not a political rival,

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.509
the President of America,

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:25.519
or an Iranian activist.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:29.590
The target was the then 41-year-old

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:32.940
best-selling author, Salman Rushdie.

00:02:33.850 --> 00:02:36.050
His crime was blasphemy.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.150
In his novel The Satanic Verses, the

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:42.630
Ayatollah declared, he had insulted

00:02:42.639 --> 00:02:44.870
Islam and the Prophet Muhammad.

00:02:45.879 --> 00:02:48.870
Now, Salman Rushdie was raised as

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:51.300
a Muslim, he was a highly educated

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.040
man, and had a strong knowledge

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:55.930
of Islamic history and culture.

00:02:56.849 --> 00:03:00.030
He knew exactly what this meant.

00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:04.439
A fatwa was not a declaration that the

00:03:04.469 --> 00:03:06.859
state of Iran would be sending its own

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:09.560
assassins, or that he would be tried

00:03:09.580 --> 00:03:12.110
and sentenced if he ever visited Iran.

00:03:13.095 --> 00:03:16.225
In this case, the fatwa functioned

00:03:16.405 --> 00:03:19.145
as a religious ruling calling on

00:03:19.145 --> 00:03:22.425
Muslims to kill Rushdie and those

00:03:22.445 --> 00:03:24.195
involved in spreading the book.

00:03:24.955 --> 00:03:27.715
Any Muslim who had the opportunity to

00:03:27.775 --> 00:03:30.085
carry out the sentence and failed to

00:03:30.085 --> 00:03:32.725
do so would be failing in their faith.

00:03:34.575 --> 00:03:36.995
This religious declaration was

00:03:36.995 --> 00:03:39.525
followed up with financial incentives.

00:03:40.495 --> 00:03:42.895
An Iranian religious foundation

00:03:43.135 --> 00:03:45.635
offered three million dollars to

00:03:45.685 --> 00:03:47.735
any Iranian citizen who killed

00:03:47.735 --> 00:03:50.565
him, and one million dollars to

00:03:50.585 --> 00:03:52.895
any non-Iranian who did the same.

00:03:54.165 --> 00:03:55.925
Within hours, the British

00:03:55.925 --> 00:03:57.805
government would place Rushdie

00:03:58.035 --> 00:04:00.045
under armed police protection.

00:04:00.685 --> 00:04:03.905
He went into hiding, and ever since,

00:04:04.165 --> 00:04:07.435
for the past four decades, his ability

00:04:07.625 --> 00:04:09.945
to live a normal life was over.

00:04:11.075 --> 00:04:13.265
This is almost certainly the most

00:04:13.265 --> 00:04:15.605
famous story of modern-day blasphemy

00:04:15.825 --> 00:04:18.735
in the English-speaking world, and asks

00:04:18.755 --> 00:04:21.065
important questions about freedom of

00:04:21.065 --> 00:04:23.815
speech, religious tolerance, and more.

00:04:24.325 --> 00:04:26.755
It’s also a long and complicated story,

00:04:27.055 --> 00:04:30.005
and I’m splitting it into two parts.

00:04:30.535 --> 00:04:33.195
Today we’ll talk about the lead up to

00:04:33.195 --> 00:04:36.125
the fatwa, and its immediate aftermath.

00:04:36.575 --> 00:04:39.305
And in the next episode we’ll talk

00:04:39.315 --> 00:04:41.395
about what has happened since, and

00:04:41.615 --> 00:04:44.095
reflect on the questions this whole

00:04:44.125 --> 00:04:46.195
episode got people talking about.

00:04:47.015 --> 00:04:50.605
So, back to Valentine’s Day, 1989.

00:04:51.135 --> 00:04:54.175
And in fact, back even further, as

00:04:54.415 --> 00:04:57.065
to understand how things had reached

00:04:57.075 --> 00:04:59.335
this point, we need to go back to

00:04:59.335 --> 00:05:01.785
the author himself, back to the book,

00:05:02.155 --> 00:05:04.985
and back to the man in Tehran whose

00:05:04.995 --> 00:05:07.515
word had just changed everything.

00:05:08.875 --> 00:05:11.255
Salman Rushdie was born in what was

00:05:11.265 --> 00:05:16.005
then Bombay on the 19th of June, 1947.

00:05:16.755 --> 00:05:20.125
This was less than two months before India

00:05:20.315 --> 00:05:22.995
gained its independence from Britain, and

00:05:23.255 --> 00:05:26.615
the India that he grew up in, this newly

00:05:26.635 --> 00:05:28.725
independent country, partitioned from

00:05:28.725 --> 00:05:31.795
Pakistan, it was still finding its shape.

00:05:33.255 --> 00:05:36.285
Rushdie was brought up as a Muslim,

00:05:36.535 --> 00:05:39.245
but not a particularly devout one.

00:05:39.965 --> 00:05:42.965
His family was very wealthy, and his

00:05:42.965 --> 00:05:45.685
father had been educated at Cambridge

00:05:45.885 --> 00:05:48.075
before becoming a successful businessman.

00:05:49.445 --> 00:05:51.785
When he was fourteen years old,

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:54.465
the young Salman Rushdie was sent

00:05:54.505 --> 00:05:56.515
to England for his education.

00:05:57.415 --> 00:05:59.745
He went to a school called Rugby,

00:06:00.075 --> 00:06:02.325
which is incidentally where the game

00:06:02.325 --> 00:06:04.655
of rugby is said to have been invented.

00:06:05.325 --> 00:06:07.965
It's one of the most famous, prestigious

00:06:08.085 --> 00:06:10.145
and expensive private schools in the

00:06:10.145 --> 00:06:13.135
country, but he was something of an

00:06:13.145 --> 00:06:16.675
outsider; he was brown-skinned, he was

00:06:16.795 --> 00:06:19.805
Indian, he was Muslim, and he wasn’t

00:06:19.845 --> 00:06:22.775
particularly sporty or anything like that.

00:06:23.955 --> 00:06:26.165
He was clever, though, and this

00:06:26.195 --> 00:06:28.325
took him to Cambridge University,

00:06:28.675 --> 00:06:30.405
where he studied history, with

00:06:30.405 --> 00:06:32.385
a focus on the history of Islam.

00:06:33.765 --> 00:06:35.995
After graduating, like many

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:37.885
of us, he wasn’t particularly

00:06:37.885 --> 00:06:39.775
sure about what to do next.

00:06:40.785 --> 00:06:42.715
He tried his hand at acting,

00:06:43.055 --> 00:06:44.165
without much success.

00:06:44.955 --> 00:06:46.425
He had always thought of himself

00:06:46.685 --> 00:06:49.305
as a gifted writer, so tried to

00:06:49.305 --> 00:06:51.675
make it as an author, but didn’t

00:06:51.685 --> 00:06:53.475
have much luck there either.

00:06:54.335 --> 00:06:57.075
Advertising, it turned out, was something

00:06:57.075 --> 00:06:59.535
he was very good at; he had a particular

00:06:59.535 --> 00:07:02.375
skill with words, and became a very

00:07:02.375 --> 00:07:04.781
successful advertising copywriter.

00:07:05.705 --> 00:07:08.565
He came up with several slogans I

00:07:08.565 --> 00:07:10.665
can still remember from my childhood,

00:07:10.865 --> 00:07:13.425
like “naughty but nice”, which was

00:07:13.425 --> 00:07:15.865
used to sell fresh cream cakes.

00:07:16.725 --> 00:07:19.375
But he always harboured greater

00:07:19.425 --> 00:07:22.355
ambitions, ambitions of becoming a

00:07:22.365 --> 00:07:25.175
professional writer, and throughout the

00:07:25.175 --> 00:07:28.655
1970s he continued to hone his craft.

00:07:30.215 --> 00:07:33.565
His first novel, Grimus, was published

00:07:33.575 --> 00:07:37.525
in 1975, but it was largely ignored.

00:07:38.885 --> 00:07:42.155
His breakthrough came in 1981 with

00:07:42.175 --> 00:07:44.415
a novel called Midnight's Children,

00:07:44.755 --> 00:07:46.565
which was a story about India

00:07:46.755 --> 00:07:48.685
at the moment of independence.

00:07:49.465 --> 00:07:51.605
It won the Booker Prize, and

00:07:51.775 --> 00:07:53.845
would later be voted the best

00:07:53.855 --> 00:07:56.255
Booker Prize winner of all time.

00:07:57.565 --> 00:07:59.325
It was also a controversial

00:07:59.335 --> 00:08:00.715
book, in parts.

00:08:01.485 --> 00:08:03.475
There was a passage about the then

00:08:03.525 --> 00:08:05.845
Indian Prime Minister Indira Gandhi

00:08:06.055 --> 00:08:08.115
that she considered defamatory.

00:08:08.675 --> 00:08:09.715
She took him to court,

00:08:10.255 --> 00:08:11.455
and ended up winning.

00:08:12.775 --> 00:08:15.335
It was his first real brush with

00:08:15.335 --> 00:08:17.715
the law, but it would be nothing

00:08:18.045 --> 00:08:20.105
compared to what would come next.

00:08:21.164 --> 00:08:24.154
In September of 1988, he

00:08:24.154 --> 00:08:26.694
published The Satanic Verses.

00:08:27.754 --> 00:08:30.604
It was written in Rushdie’s trademark

00:08:30.664 --> 00:08:33.374
magical realism style, which is a

00:08:33.384 --> 00:08:36.064
style of writing that combines the real

00:08:36.064 --> 00:08:39.394
world and magical, impossible events.

00:08:40.404 --> 00:08:42.114
If you’re familiar with the

00:08:42.114 --> 00:08:43.986
writing of authors like Gabriel

00:08:43.986 --> 00:08:46.434
García Márquez or Isabel Allende,

00:08:46.864 --> 00:08:49.644
Rushdie wrote in a similar style.

00:08:50.554 --> 00:08:53.444
This is an important detail, because

00:08:53.704 --> 00:08:55.864
it is so clearly a work of fiction;

00:08:56.164 --> 00:08:58.844
there is no way anyone could read the

00:08:58.844 --> 00:09:01.804
book and interpret it as non-fiction.

00:09:02.764 --> 00:09:06.124
So, what happens in The Satanic Verses?

00:09:06.644 --> 00:09:09.694
Well, it’s quite a long and complicated

00:09:09.694 --> 00:09:11.644
novel, not least because there are

00:09:11.914 --> 00:09:15.184
multiple storylines, and it’s often very

00:09:15.204 --> 00:09:17.294
difficult to tell what is happening in

00:09:17.294 --> 00:09:19.984
reality and what is happening in a dream.

00:09:21.259 --> 00:09:23.860
At the centre of the book are two

00:09:23.940 --> 00:09:27.107
Indian men: Gibreel and Saladin.

00:09:28.090 --> 00:09:31.410
Gibreel is a famous Bollywood actor who

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:34.560
plays gods and prophets on screen, and

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:37.980
who is slowly losing his grip on reality.

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:41.890
Saladin is a voice actor who has

00:09:41.890 --> 00:09:44.110
spent years in England trying

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:46.990
to shed his Indian identity and

00:09:47.010 --> 00:09:48.550
become as English as possible.

00:09:50.015 --> 00:09:52.515
The novel opens with the two men

00:09:52.835 --> 00:09:55.195
falling from a hijacked aeroplane

00:09:55.485 --> 00:09:57.805
that has exploded above the English

00:09:57.805 --> 00:09:59.935
Channel, which is the stretch of

00:09:59.935 --> 00:10:02.035
water between England and France.

00:10:02.965 --> 00:10:04.195
They survive.

00:10:04.512 --> 00:10:07.162
Miraculously and without explanation.

00:10:08.712 --> 00:10:12.432
What follows is a novel in two registers:

00:10:12.832 --> 00:10:15.702
the real-world story of these two men

00:10:15.922 --> 00:10:18.942
navigating London, and a series of

00:10:19.162 --> 00:10:22.312
vivid, disturbing dream sequences.

00:10:23.732 --> 00:10:26.612
And it is in these dream sequences

00:10:26.882 --> 00:10:28.432
that the controversy began.

00:10:29.515 --> 00:10:32.035
In one of Gibreel's dreams, a

00:10:32.075 --> 00:10:34.125
prophet figure is building a

00:10:34.125 --> 00:10:36.495
new religion in a desert city.

00:10:37.625 --> 00:10:39.015
The prophet's name in

00:10:39.015 --> 00:10:40.908
the dream is Mahound.

00:10:41.945 --> 00:10:44.245
Now, you don’t have to be an Islamic

00:10:44.255 --> 00:10:47.125
scholar to guess that Mahound could be

00:10:47.135 --> 00:10:49.445
a reference to the prophet Muhammad,

00:10:49.815 --> 00:10:52.545
Gibreel could be a reference to the angel

00:10:52.555 --> 00:10:56.015
Gabriel and Saladin to the Muslim military

00:10:56.015 --> 00:10:58.235
leader who defeated the crusaders.

00:10:59.115 --> 00:11:02.315
Remember, Rushdie was a Cambridge-educated

00:11:02.375 --> 00:11:04.925
scholar of Islamic history, and

00:11:05.105 --> 00:11:07.485
these names were chosen precisely

00:11:07.685 --> 00:11:09.575
because of their relevance to Islam.

00:11:10.615 --> 00:11:12.455
And Mahound was a

00:11:12.455 --> 00:11:14.655
particularly offensive choice.

00:11:15.655 --> 00:11:18.175
It was a derogatory medieval European

00:11:18.175 --> 00:11:20.115
name for the Prophet Muhammad,

00:11:20.425 --> 00:11:23.735
and was used in crusading texts to

00:11:23.765 --> 00:11:26.525
portray Islam as a false religion.

00:11:27.815 --> 00:11:30.545
Now, had that been the only

00:11:30.555 --> 00:11:33.065
offensive choice, perhaps the

00:11:33.065 --> 00:11:35.385
reaction to The Satanic Verses might

00:11:35.385 --> 00:11:37.405
have been a little less extreme.

00:11:38.455 --> 00:11:39.085
It wasn’t.

00:11:40.375 --> 00:11:42.455
The most offensive part is when

00:11:42.455 --> 00:11:45.195
Gibreel has a dream about Mahound.

00:11:46.325 --> 00:11:48.955
Within the dream, the prophet is

00:11:48.955 --> 00:11:51.625
receiving revelations from an angel.

00:11:52.385 --> 00:11:56.295
But at one crucial moment, the wrong

00:11:56.305 --> 00:11:59.465
words come through — words that allow the

00:11:59.465 --> 00:12:03.401
worship of three pre-Islamic goddesses.

00:12:03.915 --> 00:12:05.275
The prophet recites them.

00:12:05.855 --> 00:12:07.845
Then the angel returns to

00:12:07.845 --> 00:12:09.625
correct the mistake: those

00:12:09.625 --> 00:12:11.615
words did not come from God.

00:12:12.195 --> 00:12:13.605
They came from Satan,

00:12:13.785 --> 00:12:15.265
who had slipped them in.

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:18.880
Now, I don't want to get too bogged

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:21.260
down in Islamic theology here,

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:23.860
but this is not something that

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.410
Rushdie invented out of thin air.

00:12:26.930 --> 00:12:29.640
In several early Islamic sources,

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:31.600
there are accounts of the prophet

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:34.360
Muhammad receiving verses that he

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:36.950
later came to believe had not come

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:39.660
from God at all — that Satan had,

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:42.710
somehow, corrupted the revelation.

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:46.810
The story is known as the story of the

00:12:46.810 --> 00:12:50.120
gharaniq, from the Arabic word for cranes.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:53.170
It is highly contested, and

00:12:53.380 --> 00:12:55.923
many later scholars reject it.

00:12:57.170 --> 00:13:01.050
But it exists in the historical record and

00:13:01.050 --> 00:13:03.500
Rushdie, who had studied Islamic history

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:05.760
at Cambridge, he knew it was there.

00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:08.490
Hence the name of the

00:13:08.490 --> 00:13:10.600
book: The Satanic Verses.

00:13:11.630 --> 00:13:14.940
This is what made the book so explosive.

00:13:15.660 --> 00:13:18.300
Islam centres on the belief that the

00:13:18.300 --> 00:13:20.690
Quran is the literal word of God,

00:13:21.010 --> 00:13:23.910
dictated, word for word, through the

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:26.410
angel Jibreel, to the Prophet Muhammad.

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:27.760
Every word.

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:28.780
Every letter.

00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:31.240
Exactly as God intended.

00:13:32.050 --> 00:13:34.390
If someone raises even the

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:36.650
possibility that the wrong words

00:13:36.690 --> 00:13:38.740
might have been inserted, they are

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:41.170
not just asking a literary question.

00:13:41.590 --> 00:13:43.720
They are questioning the foundation

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:45.800
the entire faith rests on.

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:48.750
These weren’t the only

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:50.050
offensive parts either.

00:13:50.513 --> 00:13:52.923
Rushdie also included a dream

00:13:52.923 --> 00:13:55.754
sequence involving a brothel in

00:13:55.754 --> 00:13:57.674
which the prostitutes share the

00:13:57.674 --> 00:14:00.234
same names as the Prophet’s wives.

00:14:01.444 --> 00:14:03.744
Now, Rushdie knew this book

00:14:03.824 --> 00:14:04.864
would be controversial.

00:14:05.354 --> 00:14:07.074
He later admitted that he

00:14:07.454 --> 00:14:09.074
“expected a few mullahs would

00:14:09.084 --> 00:14:10.874
be offended and call him names”.

00:14:11.824 --> 00:14:14.224
In other words, this might offend a few

00:14:14.224 --> 00:14:16.624
religious leaders, but that would be it.

00:14:18.074 --> 00:14:20.614
For a scholar of Islamic history,

00:14:21.084 --> 00:14:22.774
he seriously misjudged it.

00:14:23.904 --> 00:14:26.204
There were some concerns flagged

00:14:26.504 --> 00:14:28.544
during the editorial process in the

00:14:28.544 --> 00:14:32.294
UK, but the British publisher, Penguin,

00:14:32.444 --> 00:14:34.794
or Viking Penguin as it was at the

00:14:34.804 --> 00:14:37.094
time, went ahead with it anyway.

00:14:38.194 --> 00:14:40.164
And by the way, in case you haven’t

00:14:40.204 --> 00:14:42.484
heard of Penguin, it’s not some

00:14:42.654 --> 00:14:45.144
small, niche publisher; it’s

00:14:45.154 --> 00:14:46.674
one of the biggest in the world.

00:14:47.854 --> 00:14:51.834
Now, the book was initially well received

00:14:51.864 --> 00:14:54.764
by critics, and won the prestigious

00:14:54.824 --> 00:14:57.244
Whitbread prize a couple of months later.

00:14:58.104 --> 00:15:00.944
Many literary critics praised the novel

00:15:01.164 --> 00:15:04.214
as a daring and ambitious work of fiction.

00:15:05.144 --> 00:15:08.344
But at the same time as literary critics

00:15:08.344 --> 00:15:10.684
were praising it, there were concerns

00:15:10.684 --> 00:15:13.284
raised by the British Muslim community.

00:15:14.424 --> 00:15:16.847
Religious leaders wrote letters to their

00:15:16.847 --> 00:15:20.404
MPs calling for the book to be withdrawn

00:15:20.444 --> 00:15:23.324
from sale because of its offensive nature.

00:15:24.744 --> 00:15:27.583
And it wasn’t just in the UK.

00:15:28.403 --> 00:15:30.834
Further abroad, especially in countries

00:15:31.144 --> 00:15:33.864
with large Muslim or Muslim-majority

00:15:33.864 --> 00:15:36.524
populations, governments went further,

00:15:36.984 --> 00:15:39.204
and the book was either rejected

00:15:39.204 --> 00:15:41.664
by publishers or outright banned.

00:15:42.254 --> 00:15:44.854
India, Pakistan, then Bangladesh,

00:15:45.014 --> 00:15:47.824
Sudan, Malaysia...the list goes on.

00:15:49.014 --> 00:15:51.724
But coming back to the UK, the

00:15:51.724 --> 00:15:54.664
reaction was particularly complicated.

00:15:55.834 --> 00:15:58.324
The country had a growing Muslim

00:15:58.324 --> 00:16:01.104
population, especially in areas of

00:16:01.104 --> 00:16:03.044
Northern England which had large

00:16:03.114 --> 00:16:04.864
Pakistani-heritage communities.

00:16:06.024 --> 00:16:08.534
The complication here wasn’t just

00:16:08.574 --> 00:16:10.364
because of the content of the

00:16:10.364 --> 00:16:12.944
book, but who had written it.

00:16:13.864 --> 00:16:17.184
Rushdie was Indian, he was a Muslim.

00:16:18.054 --> 00:16:20.444
He, of all people, he shouldn’t

00:16:20.444 --> 00:16:23.004
be insulting the prophet or doing

00:16:23.044 --> 00:16:25.094
anything that could offend or

00:16:25.094 --> 00:16:27.214
marginalise a group that already

00:16:27.214 --> 00:16:29.304
felt on the margins of society.

00:16:30.404 --> 00:16:33.244
In cities like Bradford and Birmingham,

00:16:33.624 --> 00:16:36.424
Muslim communities were facing racism,

00:16:36.884 --> 00:16:39.484
unemployment, and a persistent sense

00:16:39.664 --> 00:16:42.164
of being ignored and undervalued

00:16:42.354 --> 00:16:44.164
by mainstream British society.

00:16:45.314 --> 00:16:48.724
And there was one of them, on the face

00:16:48.724 --> 00:16:51.054
of it, insulting the prophet Mohammad.

00:16:52.264 --> 00:16:54.794
Yet, other than his country of

00:16:54.794 --> 00:16:58.044
birth, his nominal religion and the

00:16:58.044 --> 00:17:00.674
colour of his skin, he shared very

00:17:00.674 --> 00:17:02.814
little with the south Asian immigrant

00:17:02.814 --> 00:17:04.884
community of places like Bradford.

00:17:06.074 --> 00:17:07.854
He had gone to one of the most

00:17:07.864 --> 00:17:10.124
exclusive private schools in Britain.

00:17:10.664 --> 00:17:13.274
By the late 1980s, he had become

00:17:13.474 --> 00:17:15.254
one of the most establishment

00:17:15.264 --> 00:17:17.294
figures in British public life.

00:17:17.774 --> 00:17:19.544
He was a Booker Prize winner.

00:17:19.874 --> 00:17:21.684
A fixture at the best

00:17:21.694 --> 00:17:22.854
London dinner parties.

00:17:23.114 --> 00:17:25.614
Someone who moved in circles that

00:17:25.614 --> 00:17:28.194
had, for generations, treated people

00:17:28.194 --> 00:17:30.574
who looked like him as outsiders.

00:17:31.364 --> 00:17:33.924
He had used the material of South

00:17:33.954 --> 00:17:37.644
Asian life — of migration, faith and

00:17:37.644 --> 00:17:40.574
identity — he had used all of this

00:17:40.664 --> 00:17:43.384
to build his literary career, and

00:17:43.724 --> 00:17:46.674
in so doing had written words that

00:17:46.674 --> 00:17:49.124
he knew would be deeply offensive.

00:17:50.354 --> 00:17:52.464
In other words, he was a traitor.

00:17:53.587 --> 00:17:56.707
This all came to a head in January of

00:17:56.707 --> 00:18:00.947
1989, when protestors in Bradford publicly

00:18:00.947 --> 00:18:03.457
burned a copy of The Satanic Verses.

00:18:04.797 --> 00:18:08.227
To state the obvious, this was an oddity,

00:18:08.447 --> 00:18:11.067
an unusual event, in modern Britain.

00:18:12.037 --> 00:18:14.407
There were comparisons with Nazi Germany,

00:18:14.767 --> 00:18:17.427
when books by Jewish and politically

00:18:17.477 --> 00:18:19.557
undesirable authors were destroyed.

00:18:20.857 --> 00:18:23.807
Yes, it was a protest against The Satanic

00:18:23.807 --> 00:18:27.577
Verses, but it was also a protest against

00:18:27.597 --> 00:18:30.187
the perceived double standards we talked

00:18:30.187 --> 00:18:32.427
about in the previous episode, how

00:18:32.647 --> 00:18:34.937
blasphemy law protected Christianity,

00:18:35.277 --> 00:18:38.377
but offered no protections to Islam

00:18:38.427 --> 00:18:40.477
or any other religion for that matter.

00:18:41.377 --> 00:18:43.727
Now, we could continue talking

00:18:43.747 --> 00:18:45.904
about the reaction in Britain, but

00:18:46.204 --> 00:18:48.114
we need to move on to the fatwa,

00:18:48.464 --> 00:18:50.974
as this will be the defining event.

00:18:52.024 --> 00:18:54.134
We also need a little background

00:18:54.404 --> 00:18:56.394
on the man behind the fatwa,

00:18:56.914 --> 00:18:58.264
the Ayatollah Khomeini.

00:18:59.114 --> 00:19:02.044
Khomeini was born either in 1900 or

00:19:02.224 --> 00:19:05.984
1902 in a small town in central Iran.

00:19:07.034 --> 00:19:10.404
He too was a highly educated man, a

00:19:10.404 --> 00:19:13.274
man of letters, and a keen student

00:19:13.664 --> 00:19:15.774
not just of Islamic theology,

00:19:16.164 --> 00:19:18.604
but also of Western philosophy.

00:19:19.934 --> 00:19:22.984
His path was a religious one, and

00:19:23.194 --> 00:19:25.194
he eventually earned the title of

00:19:25.274 --> 00:19:28.164
ayatollah, which means "sign of God"

00:19:28.524 --> 00:19:30.784
and represents the highest level of

00:19:30.784 --> 00:19:33.274
learning in Shia Islamic scholarship.

00:19:34.404 --> 00:19:37.704
Now, traditionally, Shia clerics had

00:19:37.704 --> 00:19:40.324
stayed well away from political power.

00:19:41.214 --> 00:19:44.094
Their remit was the mosque, the

00:19:44.104 --> 00:19:46.654
madrass, and matters of religion.

00:19:47.764 --> 00:19:49.304
Over the course of his long

00:19:49.334 --> 00:19:52.484
life, however, Khomeini had come

00:19:52.484 --> 00:19:54.694
to adopt a different position.

00:19:55.634 --> 00:19:58.624
He argued that Islamic scholars had not

00:19:58.624 --> 00:20:02.084
just the right, but the duty, to govern.

00:20:03.194 --> 00:20:05.384
In a properly Islamic state,

00:20:05.844 --> 00:20:08.814
sovereignty did not belong to elected

00:20:08.854 --> 00:20:10.974
politicians or secular rulers.

00:20:11.404 --> 00:20:13.944
It belonged to religious scholars

00:20:14.174 --> 00:20:16.164
who understood divine law.

00:20:17.564 --> 00:20:20.564
Now, this made him a dangerous figure

00:20:20.894 --> 00:20:22.894
to the political leaders of Iran.

00:20:23.814 --> 00:20:25.464
He was exiled by the

00:20:25.464 --> 00:20:28.254
Western-backed Shah in 1964.

00:20:28.771 --> 00:20:31.281
He spent the next fifteen years in Iraq

00:20:31.621 --> 00:20:33.841
and then in a village outside Paris,

00:20:34.091 --> 00:20:36.671
continuing to write and teach, his

00:20:36.671 --> 00:20:38.911
voice reaching Iran through cassette

00:20:38.911 --> 00:20:40.931
tapes smuggled across the border.

00:20:42.294 --> 00:20:45.264
And when the revolution came, in

00:20:45.474 --> 00:20:49.694
1978 and 1979, it was Khomeini who

00:20:49.704 --> 00:20:52.064
emerged as its undisputed leader.

00:20:53.141 --> 00:20:57.421
In February 1979, he flew back to Tehran.

00:20:58.371 --> 00:20:59.811
Millions came out into

00:20:59.811 --> 00:21:01.141
the streets to greet him.

00:21:01.734 --> 00:21:04.124
Within two weeks, the Shah's government

00:21:04.244 --> 00:21:06.144
had collapsed, and the Islamic

00:21:06.154 --> 00:21:08.294
Republic of Iran was proclaimed.

00:21:09.734 --> 00:21:12.364
What Khomeini had created was

00:21:12.374 --> 00:21:15.194
a theocracy: a state in which

00:21:15.204 --> 00:21:17.974
religious law, as interpreted by

00:21:17.974 --> 00:21:20.774
the Supreme Leader, was supreme.

00:21:22.164 --> 00:21:24.754
Yes, there were other countries in

00:21:24.754 --> 00:21:26.994
which the law was heavily based on

00:21:26.994 --> 00:21:30.844
Islam, but Iran became the first modern

00:21:30.844 --> 00:21:33.844
country to create this kind of Islamic

00:21:33.844 --> 00:21:36.524
system ruled by religious leaders.

00:21:37.917 --> 00:21:41.217
As such, the Islamic Republic understood

00:21:41.217 --> 00:21:44.097
itself not just as the government of

00:21:44.137 --> 00:21:47.837
Iran, but as the leader of a global

00:21:47.877 --> 00:21:50.167
Islamic revolution, responsible

00:21:50.377 --> 00:21:52.817
for defending Muslims everywhere.

00:21:54.167 --> 00:21:57.527
By February 1989, however, the

00:21:57.527 --> 00:22:00.267
revolution was not in a strong position.

00:22:01.357 --> 00:22:04.057
Khomeini was almost ninety years old.

00:22:04.397 --> 00:22:05.637
He was ill.

00:22:06.067 --> 00:22:07.527
He would be dead by June.

00:22:08.887 --> 00:22:11.607
Iran was licking its wounds after

00:22:11.617 --> 00:22:14.087
a devastating eight-year conflict

00:22:14.257 --> 00:22:15.877
with Saddam Hussein’s Iraq.

00:22:16.427 --> 00:22:17.537
Hundreds of thousands

00:22:17.537 --> 00:22:19.127
of Iranians had died.

00:22:19.537 --> 00:22:21.007
The economy was struggling.

00:22:21.577 --> 00:22:22.947
There were signs of dissent.

00:22:24.027 --> 00:22:26.667
And then a British-Indian novelist

00:22:26.947 --> 00:22:29.027
published a book that was causing

00:22:29.107 --> 00:22:31.327
outrage across the Muslim world.

00:22:32.517 --> 00:22:35.044
A writer had insulted the Prophet.

00:22:35.197 --> 00:22:36.907
He had attacked Islam.

00:22:37.837 --> 00:22:39.637
Who was going to speak for the faith?

00:22:39.937 --> 00:22:42.757
Who had the authority to respond?

00:22:43.847 --> 00:22:45.797
The Supreme Leader of Iran, of course.

00:22:46.167 --> 00:22:47.237
Ayatollah Khomeini.

00:22:48.377 --> 00:22:52.027
So, the fatwa was not only about a novel.

00:22:52.374 --> 00:22:54.624
It was about who was going to stand

00:22:54.624 --> 00:22:56.934
up for the global Muslim community,

00:22:57.184 --> 00:22:59.964
who had the right to define what

00:22:59.964 --> 00:23:02.134
counted as an attack on the faith,

00:23:02.474 --> 00:23:04.594
and ultimately, whether the Islamic

00:23:04.594 --> 00:23:07.184
Republic could project its power and

00:23:07.214 --> 00:23:09.894
moral authority far beyond its borders.

00:23:11.064 --> 00:23:13.224
Khomeini clearly thought that

00:23:13.234 --> 00:23:15.464
this responsibility lay with him.

00:23:16.781 --> 00:23:19.161
Now, the fatwa was issued on

00:23:19.161 --> 00:23:21.881
the 14th of February, 1989.

00:23:22.521 --> 00:23:24.611
Within hours, Rushdie was placed

00:23:24.651 --> 00:23:26.151
under armed police protection.

00:23:26.541 --> 00:23:28.461
He was moved to a safe house.

00:23:29.001 --> 00:23:31.921
His ability to go for a walk, to call

00:23:31.921 --> 00:23:34.211
a friend without taking precautions, to

00:23:34.211 --> 00:23:36.441
make a spontaneous decision about where

00:23:36.441 --> 00:23:39.901
to have lunch, all of it ended that day.

00:23:40.711 --> 00:23:42.111
He would not live a free and

00:23:42.111 --> 00:23:44.221
open life for nearly a decade.

00:23:45.891 --> 00:23:47.841
OK then, that is it for today's

00:23:47.841 --> 00:23:50.881
episode, part two of our mini-series

00:23:50.911 --> 00:23:52.951
on blasphemy and free expression.

00:23:53.311 --> 00:23:55.661
In the next episode, we'll follow Rushdie

00:23:55.671 --> 00:23:57.921
into the years that came after: the

00:23:57.931 --> 00:24:00.011
violence against those who helped publish

00:24:00.011 --> 00:24:02.641
the book, the arguments it provoked among

00:24:02.641 --> 00:24:04.361
some of the most famous writers in the

00:24:04.361 --> 00:24:07.301
world, and an extraordinary attempt on

00:24:07.301 --> 00:24:09.881
his life more than thirty years later.

00:24:10.721 --> 00:24:12.621
You've been listening to English Learning

00:24:12.621 --> 00:24:15.231
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:24:15.611 --> 00:24:18.431
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:24:18.431 --> 00:24:20.681
I'll catch you in the next episode.

